45417] [17592186050648 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050176 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050648 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050648 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050648 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157237 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050648 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157238 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050648 #edb/ref 1031 "Relations on Finite Sets" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050648 #edb/ref 1033 2817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050648 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "5c32a7e5-b594-532c-868b-817b3d0ee933" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050882 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050882 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050882 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050882 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186059777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050882 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186059778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050882 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186059779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050882 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186059780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050882 #edb/ref 1031 "Biconditional Statements" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050882 #edb/ref 1033 248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050882 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "6e1bba25-5964-5d7f-844a-632e8e502d16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059416 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12715" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059416 #edb/ref 1071 "Express the following statement as a conjunction of implications.\n\n$$\n\\neg P \\Leftrightarrow Q\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059416 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9df9c7d3-b06a-5f0c-be20-c0d24baa81ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059416 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the definition of the biconditional, \n\n- $A \\Leftrightarrow B$ is true if $A\\Rightarrow B$ **and** $B\\Rightarrow A.$ Otherwise, it is false.\n\n- In other words, $A \\Leftrightarrow B$ is true if $A$ and $B$ have the same truth values. Otherwise, it is false.\n\nTherefore, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\neg P \\Leftrightarrow Q \\equiv (\\neg P \\Rightarrow Q) \\land (Q \\Rightarrow \\neg P).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059417 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12716" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059417 #edb/ref 1071 "What are the missing entries in the following truth table?\n\n| $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,Q\\,$ | $\\, ( \\neg P \\land Q) \\Leftrightarrow Q \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059417 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b5abe855-4626-5575-a03e-04a377eef82b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059417 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the definition of the biconditional, \n\n- $A \\Leftrightarrow B$ is true if $A\\Rightarrow B$ **and** $B\\Rightarrow A.$ Otherwise, it is false.\n\n- In other words, $A \\Leftrightarrow B$ is true if $A$ and $B$ have the same truth values. Otherwise, it is false.\n\nFirst, we add columns for $\\neg P$ and $\\neg P \\land Q{:}$\n\n| $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,Q\\,$ | $\\, \\neg P \\,$ | $\\, \\neg P \\land Q \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n\nFinally, we add a column for $(\\neg P \\land Q) \\Leftrightarrow Q.$ \n\n| $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,Q\\,$ | $\\, \\neg P \\,$ | $\\, \\neg P \\land Q \\,$ | $\\, (\\neg P \\land Q) \\Leftrightarrow Q \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059418 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12717" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059418 #edb/ref 1071 "If $A$ is true and $B$ is true, what is the truth value of $A \\Leftrightarrow B ?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059418 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "15d6b6c0-af70-5d22-bfdc-b2c0aaf98de5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059418 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the definition of the biconditional, \n\n- $A \\Leftrightarrow B$ is true if $A\\Rightarrow B$ **and** $B\\Rightarrow A.$ Otherwise, it is false.\n\n- In other words, $A \\Leftrightarrow B$ is true if $A$ and $B$ have the same truth values. Otherwise, it is false.\n\nSo, when $A = \\textrm T$ and $B = \\textrm T,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A⇔B) & =(\\text{T}⇔\\text{T}) \\\\  & =\\text{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe complete truth table is shown below:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,A \\Leftrightarrow B\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  |  |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059419 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12718" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059419 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are equivalent to $\\lnot (\\neg Y \\Leftrightarrow Z)?$\n\n- $Y \\Rightarrow \\lnot Z$\n\n- $(Y \\lor Z) \\Rightarrow (Y \\land Z)$\n\n- $(Y \\land Z) \\land (Y \\land \\lnot Z)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059419 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "51dc6bfc-d581-5972-9101-a80e614b33e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059419 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we use the definition of the biconditional statement to write the equivalence as a conjunction of implications: \n\n$$\n\\lnot (\\lnot Y \\Leftrightarrow Z) \n\\equiv \\lnot \\big[(\\lnot Y \\Rightarrow Z) \\land (Z \\Rightarrow \\lnot Y)\\big]\n$$\n\nNow, we represent all the implications using disjunctions and negations:\n\n$$\n\\lnot \\big[(\\lnot Y \\Rightarrow Z) \\land (Z \\Rightarrow \\lnot Y)\\big]\n\\equiv \\lnot \\big[(\\lnot \\lnot Y \\lor Z) \\land (\\lnot Z \\lor \\lnot Y)\\big]\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify the double negations:\n\n$$\n\\lnot \\big[(\\lnot \\lnot Y \\lor Z) \\land (\\lnot Z \\lor \\lnot Y)\\big] \n\\equiv \\lnot \\big[(Y \\lor Z) \\land (\\lnot Z \\lor \\lnot Y)\\big]\n$$\n\nNow, we use De Morgan's laws:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬[(Y∨Z)∧(¬Z∨¬Y)] & ≡¬(Y∨Z)∨¬(¬Z∨¬Y) \\\\  & ≡¬(Y∨Z)∨¬¬(Z∧Y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we again simplify the double negations and write down symbols in alphabetic order inside the parentheses using commutative laws:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(Y∨Z)∨¬¬(Z∧Y) & ≡¬(Y∨Z)∨(Z∧Y) \\\\  & ≡¬(Y∨Z)∨(Y∧Z) \\\\  & ≡(Y∨Z)⇒(Y∧Z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is only II." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059777 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059777 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186060144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059777 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186060145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059777 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186379202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059777 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186379215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059777 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186379316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059777 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186379322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059777 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059777 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059777 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412690 #edb/ref 17592186045417